WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.260
You are watching ColdFusion TV.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.880
This video is the second episode of a new series based on my book "New Thinking,"

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.700
which is basically the grand history of science and technology from the Industrial Revolution till today.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.680
This story is about something I find pretty interesting.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.080
It's about Gary Kildall, the man who should have been Bill Gates.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.080
It's an absolutely fascinating story, and I think that more people should know about it.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.200
The year is 1977,

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.600
and Apple had just become extremely successful with their first PC, the Apple II.

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:50.359
It was the first mass production personal computer that you didn't have to assemble yourself.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:58.249
JB wanted to know why I'm doing better than all the other managers. It's no secret. I have an Apple.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.260
Sure there's a big computer three flights down,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.560
but it won't test my options, do my charts, or edit my reports like my Apple.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:11.100
Move words, like that, or you can move whole paragraphs around to anywhere you want them. It's amazing.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:17.960
You can use the very same Apple to do a zillion other things. You could say that the Apple is mightier than the pen.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.320
Apple: The Personal Computer.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.160
PC industry was already worth 1 billion dollars just three years after it had begun.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.040
For the first time, computers amplified productivity and human intellect from within the home.

00:01:35.219 --> 00:01:39.889
IBM, who was in the corporate computer business of selling large mainframes, just saw the dollar signs.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.589
This was the opportunity of all opportunities.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:49.399
By 1980, IBM had turned their attention to Apple’s success in the PC market, and realized that they were wrong about the PC.

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:52.729
It wasn't just a hobbyist toy anymore. It could actually be useful.

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:59.569
The problem for IBM is that they were a huge company. They were bureaucratic and very slow when it came to decisions.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:02.779
It was going to take years for them just to come up with a PC design,

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.149
so how were they going to compete in a timely manner?

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.080
The answer came in the form of a secret small team within the company.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.850
This small team was assigned the task to create a business-orientated personal computer.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:19.420
When they got to work, the decision was made to use off-the-shelf parts to speed up the design process.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.860
Using non-IBM parts was very unusual for the company.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.949
With this method, instead of building a computer from scratch, they managed to have a complete product within a year.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.789
In 1980, IBM had the PC hardware cobbled together.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.860
The only thing needed was software to run on it. What IBM needed was an operating system.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.620
An operating system is like a computer's digital traffic cop.

00:02:41.629 --> 00:02:48.529
It keeps track of how files are stored and how the computer handles hardware such as a mouse, screen, floppy disk drive, and etc.

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:52.620
Basically, it's the basis that allows for a modern computer to run.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.519
At this point, this is where the story starts heating up.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:01.759
IBM was the biggest tech company of the time, so any PC that they made would have an incredible impact on the world.

00:03:01.909 --> 00:03:06.949
So what software was going to run on it? This was the battle for one operating system that would rule them all.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:15.140
The stakes are high. Every decision here impacts the rest of all history and herein lies one of the saddest stories in computing history.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:24.220
While building their PC in 1980, IBM approached Microsoft to build the operating system.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:31.760
Before saying a word about the project, IBM asked Bill Gates, who was initially mistaken for the office intern, to sign a nondisclosure agreement.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:36.280
Bill Gates had to keep IBM's plan a secret and he couldn't tell a single soul.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:41.460
When IBM told Gates what they were doing, he remarked that Microsoft didn't have an operating system.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.780
The 25-year-old programmer would point IBM in the direction of Gary Kildall.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.519
Gary was a meek and mild-mannered man, but despite being unassuming,

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.460
he had already paved the way for operating systems and personal computers.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:58.960
In 1971, Kildall had made a programming language for Intel's first CPU, the 4004.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:04.780
But he quickly realized that there had to be a way to control how the chip interacted with the rest of the computer system.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.780
In 1972, he solved this problem with CP/M, which stands for Control Program for Microcomputers.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:14.269
This was the very first PC operating system.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.709
Before CP/M, each computer had to have tailor-made software.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:25.609
Imagine it like this. It would be like having different types of fuel for every single car model in existence.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:33.049
With an operating system like CP/M, a programmer only needed to write software just once, and CP/M would take care of the rest.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.949
It was a way to run the very same software on different computers.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:42.120
It just, uh, was a very natural thing to write, and it turns out people had s- a need for an operating system like that.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.140
And so, uh, it was a very natural thing. I wrote it for my own use.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.480
Despite this, Gary didn't really have much interest in business matters, and was just doing this as a hobby,

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:55.829
but his wife, Dorothy, convinced him to start a business and start licensing his creation.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:03.599
The result was a company called Digital Research. By 1979, Digital Research became the industry standard for operating systems.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:09.690
In essence, they were the Microsoft of the late 1970s, and Gary was equivalent to Bill Gates.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:18.800
So IBM had just approached Bill Gates, and asked him if he could make an operating system for their new PC.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.460
And Bill Gates pointed them in the direction of Gary.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.460
So, keen to waste no time,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:27.060
IBM takes Bill's advice and pays a visit to Gary in Seattle.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.560
Bill calls Gary on the phone to give him some warning that someone is coming to visit.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.440
Because of the non-disclosure agreement, Gates couldn't reveal exactly who was coming to visit.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.160
Bill exclaimed, "Treat them right, they're important guys!"

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:48.040
Unfortunately, Gary was a somewhat chilled-out guy and didn't really get the full urgency of what Bill was warning.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.920
Perhaps he thought it was just another small company, and not the largest tech company on the planet.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:58.440
Because of this, Gary isn't home when IBM visits. He's out flying one of his private planes on business.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.540
IBM ends up talking to Gary's wife, Dorothy, who is now head of operations in Digital Research.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:11.480
The lawyers of IBM begin pushing her to sign a nondisclosure agreement, essentially to say that they were never there.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.200
Dorothy wasn't impressed by this, and refused to sign the document.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:18.960
The IBM team had a short temper. After going nowhere with negotiations,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:22.300
they became frustrated and decided to leave Gary's house.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.180
A few days later, IBM would approach Bill Gates a second time.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:31.709
Gates, being deterministic and opportunistic in nature, was never going to give Gary a second chance.

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:37.499
He saw that IBM had the potential to change the PC market into something entirely different:

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:41.310
a new cleaned-up business image instead of the geeky enthusiast image it had.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:46.170
So here comes the clincher. Bill Gates decided to do something pretty sneaky:

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.770
he told IBM that Microsoft could in fact make an operating system, even though they didn't have one.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:59.440
What Microsoft decided to do was just buy an operating system from a small company down the road for $75,000.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:05.020
This operating system was called the Quick and Dirty Operating System, or QDOS for short.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.860
If you think that that's a bit of a weird name, there's a reason for it.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.940
The code it used for the software was essentially a ripoff of CP/M, the operating system that Gary had made.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:21.080
So with this ripoff of CP/M, Microsoft now had their hands on a functioning operating system.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:27.220
QDOS would become Microsoft DOS, or MS-DOS for short, and would be packaged with every IBM PC.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.960
So if you've ever used MS-DOS, it could be said that you were using the Microsoft Dirty Operating System.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.400
However, this name would be officially changed to Microsoft Disk Operating System.

00:07:36.409 --> 00:07:41.160
And of course, MS-DOS would eventually become Microsoft Windows.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.780
The first IBM PC was released in August of 1981.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:53.420
It was predicted to make 250,000 sales, but it was a smash hit, selling two million units in a couple of years.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:58.200
IBM had now overtaken Apple as the world's largest PC manufacturer.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:05.860
With this event, it was now truly ok for the white collar class to get into PCs. It was no longer for hobbyist freaks and enthusiasts.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:14.680
Around this time,/  Gary became the co-host of the show Computer Chronicles, a TV program that followed in detail the progress of personal computers at the time.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:18.260
Gary, we're going to be talking about things like transportables and lap portables

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:21.860
but I want to show you this portable over here. This looks like a pretty small computer,

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:25.400
but in fact, this is just the keyboard. I can program my things into my computer.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:30.420
In effect, this is the computer. And of course it is now a wrist portable.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:35.900
Now, I don't know whether this does anything very useful or not. In fact, that's my question about this whole subject of portables:

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:39.320
Is this another case of technology in search of a purpose?

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:45.160
I don't think so, Stuart. It's just a new dimension in technology. We have had faster processors, more memory and so forth, in the past.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:48.740
Now what we're looking for is trying to get all that speed and power into a smaller package.

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:53.350
So everything was falling into place for Bill Gates.

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:55.690
He was getting set to be the richest man in the world.

00:08:55.850 --> 00:09:01.180
The Dirty Operating System was one half of the equation, but some smart business sense would complete it.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:10.160
Because the IBM PC was made from off-the-shelf parts, other manufacturers such as Compaq and HP began making their own PC clones with the very same parts.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:18.960
The deal for Microsoft was that MS-DOS would be licensed to IBM by Microsoft for a one-time fee of $50,000, but there was a catch:

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:23.140
Microsoft never mentioned to IBM that their deal was non-exclusive.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:30.669
Soon Microsoft was selling MS-DOS to all of IBM's competitors, taking a licensing fee from every computer sold.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.170
This licensing deal has been called the greatest deal in history.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.200
It made Bill Gates a billionaire.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:43.750
Gary's failure to not be there at the right time is conversely called one of the biggest business failures in history.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:48.880
Once the sales of the IBM PC took off, Gary had realized what he had lost.

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:54.080
For a rare moment, he would shed off his kind nature and threatened to sue IBM.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.880
In a settlement, IBM agreed to offer CP/M alongside MS-DOS with every PC sold.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:07.820
Gary Kildall was pleased. People could now choose for themselves which software they liked best. Justice had finally been served.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:18.340
There was only just one problem, though. When both software packages were released, MS-DOS sold for $40 and CP/M, $240.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:22.220
This was a complete disaster for almost identical pieces of software.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:27.860
For this reason, Gary's CP/M software would fade into obscurity by the late 1980s.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.460
Gary had lost to a clone of his own creation.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:38.780
Sadly, he didn't take it well. He was so crushed by the events that he didn't ever bother suing Microsoft or IBM again.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:46.320
The strain from missing out on the greatest opportunity of many lifetimes would eventually cause Dorothy to divorce Gary.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:49.200
He would also shy away from his show, The Computer Chronicles.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:55.860
The ubiquity of personal computers in the following years meant that Gary would forever be reminded of his failure everywhere he turned.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:00.180
Kildall would slip into a bout of depression and alcoholism.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:06.240
Sadly, in 1994, Gary would die from head injuries in a fistfight at a biker bar.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:15.790
Today, Gary, the man who invented the operating system for personal computers, is only a faint footnote in technology history.

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:20.409
So I think that it's important that we keep his contribution to the evolution of computing alive.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.240
I can see that it was just a series of unfortunate events for Gary,

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:29.140
but if we can take any lessons away from them, it might be, make the most of every opportunity.

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:40.640
This was the very first episode of The Computer Chronicles, broadcast back in 1983.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:46.080
When we first launched the series, sitting by my side as co-host was Gary Kildall.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.680
But Gary was a lot more than a TV host,

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:53.940
Gary, in fact, was one of the most important individuals in the history of personal computing.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:59.220
Gary died last year, leaving a legacy not matched by many in this field.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:01.240
That's the end of that story.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:06.240
It's pretty sad note, but I thought I'd just share it with you cause it's pretty unknown, I think.

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:08.820
A lot of people don't know about Gary Kildall and what he did, so.

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:12.610
Thanks for watching. This has been Dagogo, you've been watching ColdFusion.

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:18.840
Feel free to subscribe if you just stumbled across this channel and I'll catch you again soon for the next video. Cheers guys, have a good one.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:22.360
ColdFusion, it's New Thinking.

